{"mathdb_id": 329458, "uuid": "fd1a0ff8-d5ce-4cd5-b3bf-ff82167e4807", "canonical_url": "https://mathdb.com/p/329458/alavi-erdos-malde-schwenk-conjecture-on-unimodality-for-tree", "title": "Alavi–Erdős–Malde–Schwenk conjecture on unimodality for trees and forests", "statement": "A tree is a connected acyclic graph, and a forest is an acyclic graph. For a graph $G$, its independence sequence $(i_0(G),i_1(G),\ldots,i_{\alpha(G)}(G))$ records the numbers of independent sets of each cardinality. **Alavi–Erdős–Malde–Schwenk conjecture.** Every tree and every forest has a unimodal independence sequence. The paper states that this conjecture remains open, in contrast with the disproved unimodality conjecture for all well-covered graphs.", "statement_completeness": "full", "post_type": "conjecture", "status": "open", "tags": ["graph-theory", "independence-polynomials", "unimodality"], "importance": "corpus", "source_name": "Jonathan Cutler and Luke Pebody, “Maximal-clique partitions and the Roller Coaster Conjecture”, arXiv:1412.4595 (2014).", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.4595", "first_stated_year": 2011, "first_stated_year_source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.4595", "documented_by_year": null, "documented_by_source_url": null, "references": "**3 papers in this index state this conjecture** (2011–2014). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are [arXiv:1312.6524](https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6524), [arXiv:1110.3760](https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.3760).

